-- Node: para-sbp (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"result","module":5,"title":"SBP Treatment Pathway","body":"**Suspected spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP): ascitic PMN >=250 cells/mm3.** Start treatment even with a negative Gram stain or culture. SBP means infected ascites without a surgically treatable source; CT without free air/abscess supports but does not alone exclude secondary peritonitis. [1,5]\n\n**ANTIBIOTICS NOW**\nObtain blood cultures and bedside ascitic cultures without delaying treatment. For community-acquired SBP without resistant-organism risk, give [Ceftriaxone](#/drug/ceftriaxone/sbp) **2 g IV every 24 hours**, typically 5-7 days, then tailor to cultures and clinical response. Healthcare-associated infection, shock, prior resistant isolates or recent antibiotics require broader local-protocol coverage and infectious-disease input. Avoid routine aminoglycosides because of nephrotoxicity. [1,5]\n\n**ALBUMIN TO PROTECT RENAL FUNCTION**\nGive [Albumin](#/drug/albumin/sbp) **1.5 g/kg IV within 6 hours of diagnosis, then 1 g/kg IV on day 3**. Benefit is clearest with renal dysfunction or hepatic decompensation; classic high-risk thresholds are creatinine >1 mg/dL, BUN >30 mg/dL, or bilirubin >4 mg/dL. AASLD 2021 recommends albumin with antibiotics for SBP; these thresholds should not be used to exclude all other patients automatically. Use estimated dry weight/local pharmacy protocol and monitor for pulmonary edema. [1,3,5,6]\n\n**CASE APPLICATION**\nPMN 410, creatinine 1.6, BUN 34 and bilirubin 4.6: admit and give IV ceftriaxone plus albumin. At an 80 kg dosing weight: **120 g today; 80 g on day 3**. Tense ascites can inflate scale weight, so confirm dosing weight. Sort et al. studied cefotaxime plus albumin: renal impairment 33% to 10%; in-hospital mortality 29% to 10%. [6]\n\n**MONITOR / REASSESS**\nTrack BP, urine output, creatinine, electrolytes, oxygenation and mental status; reassess diuretics in AKI/hypotension. Repeat diagnostic tap at about 48 hours when response is inadequate, resistance is possible or secondary peritonitis is suspected. PMN decline <25% suggests treatment failure: reassess coverage and obtain source evaluation. Susceptible isolate plus clear improvement may make repeat tap unnecessary. Arrange secondary prophylaxis and hepatology follow-up after recovery. [1,5]\n\n**DRAINAGE IS A SEPARATE DECISION**\nAn arbitrary 8-liter tap is not a substitute for SBP antibiotics and albumin. Therapeutic drainage is not absolutely contraindicated during SBP if tense ascites requires relief; individualize with hemodynamic/renal monitoring and coordinate albumin replacement, avoiding duplicate orders. [5]","citation":[1,3,5,6],"recommendation":"SBP is time-sensitive infection; do not wait for culture positivity.","confidence":"definitive","summary":"PMN >=250: antibiotics, admission, albumin when indicated, assess sepsis/AKI.","safetyLevel":"critical"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'para-sbp' AND tree_id = 'paracentesis';

